{{!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     icons.hbs — THE icon set. One partial, one <svg>, zero third parties.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Usage:

       {{> icons name="arrow-right"}}                       1em, inherits colour
       {{> icons name="graduation-cap" class="h-4 w-4"}}     any size/colour
       {{> icons name="rss" title="RSS feed"}}               labelled, not hidden

     Params
       name   required. An entry below. An unknown name draws nothing —
              deliberately, so a typo degrades to blank space rather than to a
              broken glyph box.
       class  Extra classes on the <svg>. The svg ALWAYS carries `.ns-icon`
              (2-components/icon.css), which sizes it to 1em square, aligns it
              on the text baseline and stops it shrinking in a flex row — so an
              icon dropped into a sentence or a button behaves like the icon
              font it replaced, with no classes at all. Pass `h-4 w-4` and so
              on to override; Tailwind utilities are the last layer, so they
              always win over the base.
       title  If given, the icon is exposed to assistive tech with this label
              (role="img" + aria-label). Omit it for decorative icons — which
              is nearly all of them, because the adjacent text already says it.

     WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
     The theme used to draw icons with a subsetted Phosphor webfont
     (<i class="ph-fill ph-rocket-launch">). That works, but it costs a font
     request before a single icon paints, renders empty boxes for any glyph
     missing from the subset, needs a Python re-subsetting step whenever a
     template wants a new icon, and puts a third party in the render path.
     Inline SVG has none of those properties: it ships inside the HTML, paints
     with the first frame, inherits currentColor, and adding an icon is adding
     a <path> here.

     THE DRAWING CONTRACT — every icon obeys it, which is what makes a row of
     them look like one set rather than a collection:

       · 24×24 viewBox, artwork inside a ~3px margin so icons optically match
         at the same class size.
       · Stroked, not filled: stroke-width 1.7, round caps and joins. The 1.7
         is the design system's own icon weight (see the inline data: icons in
         ds/components/css) and it is what makes an icon read as a sibling of
         a hairline border rather than as a blob.
       · currentColor. Never a hard-coded fill. Colour comes from the text
         colour of whatever contains it, so `text-brand-500` just works.
       · The handful of BRAND MARKS at the bottom (GitHub, LinkedIn, X,
         Facebook) are the one exception — they are drawn solid, because that
         is how those marks are specified, and they override fill/stroke on
         the path itself rather than on the <svg>.

     Names track Phosphor's vocabulary where the theme already used it, so the
     migration off the font was mechanical and so an icon is easy to guess.
     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<svg class="ns-icon{{#if class}} {{class}}{{/if}}"
     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"
     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
     {{#if title}}role="img" aria-label="{{title}}"{{else}}aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"{{/if}}>

